course: dictionary
lesson: 8503
title: Saying the opposite
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Sarcastically means saying something in a mocking way, where you say the opposite of what you mean. The words can sound nice, but the tone and the situation show the real meaning.
explain: Look at what just happened. If someone's bike gets a flat tyre and they say «Oh, great!», they do not mean it is great. The situation tells you they mean the opposite. Words said sarcastically often come with a sneer or a sigh in the voice.
explain: The words may be the same as a happy message, so always check the clues. Sarcasm can hurt feelings, so think before you use it, and be careful when you read it in a message. In the example «Case closed», said sarcastically, the speaker means the matter is not settled at all.
fact: Sarcastically is an adverb, so it tells you how something is said.
fact: «Sarcastically» means in a mocking way, saying the opposite of what you mean.
fact: Sarcasm is a kind of irony, often shown by tone of voice.
fact: In writing, «she said sarcastically» tells you not to take the words literally.
fact: «Case closed», said sarcastically, means the matter is not settled, even though the words say it is.
exercise: «Oh, brilliant, it's raining on sports day», she said sarcastically. What did she really mean? | She was singing a song | *It was a bad thing, the opposite of brilliant | It was wonderful weather
exercise: What does sarcastically tell the reader about the words that were said? | *They mean the opposite of what the words say | They should be taken exactly as written | They are a question | They were whispered very quietly
exercise: Which speech is most likely to be sarcastic after a bike crash? | *Oh, brilliant, a flat tyre, just what I needed | Ouch, that hurt a lot | Let's go to the shop | I am going to fix it now
test: What does sarcastically mean? | To say something kindly and gently | To write in a hurry | To shout very loudly | *To say the opposite of what you mean, in a mocking way
test: When a reader sees «she said sarcastically», what should they do? | Believe the words exactly as written | Copy the words down | Ignore the words completely | *Think the speaker may mean the opposite
exam: Why can sarcasm hurt feelings? | *It sounds like a joke but can be unkind | It is always said loudly | It only happens in books | It means the speaker is lost
